  "report": {
    "incident_id": "airtable-batch-042",
    "outcome": "probable_cause",
    "hypotheses": [
      {
        "description": "Most likely cause for the observed cedar failures is Airtable batch-size enforcement in the executor path. The failed production write attempted 25 records while telemetry on that same execution reports max_records_per_call=10, and the synthetic controlled comparison passes when reduced to 10 records. This supports a bounded diagnosis of provider batch record limit for the observed failed executions, not a platform-wide Airtable outage.",
        "supporting_evidence": [
          "ev_f912f7b4edda",
          "ev_fa1d3be17388",
          "ev_11cdd22233bb",
          "ev_ef1f54c4e505"
        ],
        "contradicting_evidence": [
          "ev_88a89b675c23",
          "ev_e4f48313b394"
        ],
        "confidence": "high",
        "assumptions": [
          "Scope is limited to observed cedar failures in this incident window.",
          "The synthetic reproduce comparison is supportive but not canonical production proof.",
          "Empty deployment searches weaken release-attribution, not the immediate batch-limit mechanism."
        ]
      },
      {
        "description": "A generic provider payload-size limit is a weaker alternative. Larger requests fail and smaller ones pass, but the supplied telemetry identifies a concrete record-count cap rather than payload-byte measurements or provider payload-limit error text.",
        "supporting_evidence": [
          "ev_f912f7b4edda",
          "ev_fa1d3be17388"
        ],
        "contradicting_evidence": [
          "ev_f912f7b4edda",
          "ev_fa1d3be17388"
        ],
        "confidence": "low",
        "assumptions": [
          "Record count may correlate with payload size, but no serialized byte-size telemetry is provided."
        ]
      },
      {
        "description": "A recent executor-release regression remains unconfirmed. The incident narrative mentions a recent release, but canonical deployment searches do not show a tool-executor change in the relevant window, and the supplied logs do not include executor version or validator detail needed to tie the 422s to a specific build.",
        "supporting_evidence": [
          "ev_fa1d3be17388"
        ],
        "contradicting_evidence": [
          "ev_88a89b675c23",
          "ev_e4f48313b394",
          "ev_f912f7b4edda"
        ],
        "confidence": "low",
        "assumptions": [
          "An unrecorded rollout or config drift could exist, but it is not evidenced here.",
          "The observed 422 could still come from internal validation logic, but no canonical versioned validator trace is supplied."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "recommended_actions": [
      "Use provider_batch_record_limit_remediation for this Airtable action: split updates into batches of at most 10 records, respect provider rate limits, and verify partial completion before retrying. This is a proposed next step and has not been executed.",
      "Collect missing canonical telemetry on the failing executor path: executor version/build, exact validation error text, and any outbound-attempt markers for AIRTABLE_UPDATE_MULTIPLE_RECORDS.",
      "Request a tighter synthetic reproduction that keeps Airtable configuration constant while varying only record count, to confirm whether count alone explains the 422.",
      "If failures persist even at 10 records, collect serialized payload-byte measurements and compare failing versus successful requests before elevating provider_payload_limit.",
      "Monitor cedar backlog, failure rate, and latency until the queue depth and blocked operations return to baseline; current impact is based on synthetic telemetry and observed records only."
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "Synthetic reproduce observations are supportive only; they are not canonical production executions.",
      "No supplied canonical log includes executor version, full validator error text, or outbound Airtable request markers.",
      "Deployment searches returning no executor records do not prove no unrecorded change existed; they only mean release attribution is unverified from supplied evidence."
    ]
  },
